Based on my search results, I found relevant information about custom derive macros and tuple structs in both C++ and Rust. The question specifically asks about "derive macro" which is a C++ template metaprogramming concept (Rust calls these "procedural macros"). Let me provide a comprehensive answer.

In C++, when working with unnamed tuple structs (or `std::tuple` types), you can access fields by position using `std::get<N>` or `std::tuple_element_t<N, T>`. Here's how to implement a custom derive macro that prints tuple fields by position:

## Solution for C++ Derive Macro with Unnamed Tuple Fields

The key is to use `std::tuple_element_t` and `std::get<N>` to access elements by index rather than name:

```cpp
#include <tuple>
#include <iostream>

// Custom derive macro that prints tuple fields by position
template<typename T, typename... Args>
struct PrintTupleStruct {
    // Get the type of each element
    template<typename... Ets>
    static auto get_types() {
        return std::tuple<decltype(std::get<0>(T)), 
                        decltype(std::get<1>(T)), ...>;
    }

    // Print all elements by position
    template<typename... Es>
    static void print_by_position() {
        // For each element, get its type and value
        // std::get<N>(t) returns the Nth element by position
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < sizeof...(Es); ++i) {
            const auto& element = std::get<i, T>();
            std::cout << "Element " << i << ": ";
            // Use stringify or similar to print the value
            std::cout << element;
        }
    }
};

// Example usage with an unnamed tuple struct
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

// Apply the derive macro
PrintTupleStruct<Point> point;

int main() {
    Point p{10, 20};

    // The macro will generate code that prints:
    // Element 0: 10
    // Element 1: 20

    PrintTupleStruct<Point>::print_by_position();

    return 0;
}
```

## Key C++ Techniques

1. **`std::tuple_element_t<N, T>`** - Gets the type of the Nth element in a tuple
2. **`std::get<N>(t)`** - Retrieves the value at position N from a tuple
3. **Template metaprogramming** - Used to generate the print code at compile time

## For Rust (Procedural Macros)

If you're actually working with **Rust** (where "derive macro" is the standard term), the approach is similar but uses different syntax:

```rust
use proc_macro::TokenStream;
use quote::quote;

#[derive(PrettyPrint)]
struct Point(i32, i32);

impl PrettyPrint for Point {
    fn print_by_position(&self) {
        // Access by index using .0, .1, etc. or via tuple_element
        println!("Element 0: {}", self.0);
        println!("Element 1: {}", self.1);
    }
}
```

## Summary

The core idea is to **access tuple elements by their positional index** using `std::get<N>` (C++) or `.0`, `.1` etc. (Rust) rather than by name. In a derive macro, you iterate over the tuple elements and use these indexing techniques to print each field by its position.

